5. PROTOCOL ANALYSIS
5.1 ARP – Address Resolution Protocol

ARP maps an IPv4 address to a MAC address within a local network.

Working:

A device checks its ARP cache.
If the MAC address is unknown, it broadcasts an ARP request.
The device with the matching IP address responds.
The sender stores the mapping in its ARP cache.

Wireshark Filter: arp

5.2 DNS – Domain Name System

DNS translates domain names into IP addresses.

For example, a client requests the IP address associated with a website domain.

Wireshark Filter: dns

5.3 DHCP – Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol

DHCP automatically assigns network configuration to devices.

The four main stages are:

Discover
Offer
Request
Acknowledge

Wireshark Filter: dhcp

5.4 TCP – Transmission Control Protocol

TCP provides reliable and ordered data transmission.

It uses acknowledgements, sequence numbers, and retransmissions.

Three-way handshake:

SYN
SYN-ACK
ACK

Wireshark Filter: tcp

5.5 UDP – User Datagram Protocol

UDP provides connectionless communication with low overhead.

It is commonly used in DNS, voice communication, and streaming applications.

Wireshark Filter: udp

5.6 HTTP – Hypertext Transfer Protocol

HTTP is an application-layer protocol used for communication between web clients and servers.

It supports methods such as GET, POST, PUT, and DELETE.

Wireshark Filter: http

5.7 HTTPS and TLS

HTTPS uses TLS to protect HTTP communication through encryption and authentication.

Encrypted application data generally cannot be read directly from an ordinary packet capture.

Wireshark Filter: tls

5.8 ICMP – Internet Control Message Protocol

ICMP is used for network diagnostics and error reporting.

The ping command commonly uses ICMP Echo Request and Echo Reply messages.

Wireshark Filter: icmp

